### What problem does a CDN solve?

Network latency across continents is dominated by the speed of light. A CDN places edge servers in many locations and serves cached copies from the nearest edge. Ideal for static assets and cacheable responses.

```text
  Without CDN: Mumbai origin → Chennai user = 200ms round trip
  With CDN:    Chennai edge → Chennai user = 10ms round trip
```

### What problem does Redis solve?

Redis caches dynamic data close to the application, avoiding slow DB queries or expensive recomputation. Sub-millisecond network hop in the same data center.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;

public class BalanceCache {
    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        String cached = redis.opsForValue().get("balance:" + accountId);
        if (cached != null) return new BigDecimal(cached); // cache hit: ~0.5ms

        BigDecimal balance = accountRepo.findBalance(accountId); // cache miss: ~5ms DB query
        redis.opsForValue().set("balance:" + accountId, balance.toString(), Duration.ofSeconds(30));
        return balance;
    }
}
```

### How are they complementary?

| Aspect | CDN | Redis |
|---|---|---|
| Distance optimized | User ↔ content | Application ↔ data |
| Data type | Static (images, JS, CSS) | Dynamic (sessions, balances) |
| Location | Edge (geographically distributed) | Data center (same DC as app) |
| Use together | Serves static shell from edge | Serves personalized data from DC |

You would NOT cache a private balance at public CDN edges, and you would NOT use Redis to serve video globally.

### What is a semantic cache?

For LLM applications, traditional caching keys on exact input match. A semantic cache stores an embedding for each cached question and matches by cosine similarity.

```java
package com.bank.ai;

public class SemanticCache {
    private final VectorStore vectorStore;
    private final EmbeddingModel embedder;
    private final double threshold;

    public String getCachedAnswer(String question) {
        float[] embedding = embedder.embed(question);
        SearchResult closest = vectorStore.findNearest(embedding);
        if (closest.getScore() >= threshold) {
            return closest.getAnswer(); // paraphrase hit!
        }
        return null; // cache miss → call LLM
    }

    public void cacheAnswer(String question, String answer) {
        float[] embedding = embedder.embed(question);
        vectorStore.store(embedding, answer);
    }
}
```

### What is the similarity threshold trade-off?

- Too low → returns answers for loosely related questions (wrong answers).
- Too high → rarely matches paraphrases (useless cache).
- Tune against real data; combine with guardrails (only cache stable, non-personalized answers).

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: caching personalized answers without tenant scoping
// User A asks about their loan; User B asks a similar question → gets User A's answer!

// CORRECT: scope semantic cache per tenant
vectorStore.store(embedding, answer, tenantId); // tenant-scoped
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- CDN, Redis, and semantic cache can all appear in one system at different layers.
- CDN must never cache authenticated, user-specific responses at shared edges.
- Semantic caches need TTL because underlying knowledge changes.
- The unifying idea: all caches store the result of expensive work and reuse it; they differ in which distance they shrink and how they match.

### How do you invalidate each cache layer?

Each layer has a different invalidation strategy. CDN: purge API or TTL expiry. Redis: explicit delete on write plus TTL. Semantic cache: TTL plus version tagging when underlying knowledge changes.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class CacheInvalidation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CDN invalidation:");
        System.out.println("  - TTL-based: cache headers (max-age=3600)");
        System.out.println("  - Purge API: cdn.purge('/static/rates.js')");
        System.out.println("  - Versioned URLs: /static/rates.v42.js (cache-bust)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Redis invalidation:");
        System.out.println("  - Explicit delete on write: redis.delete('balance:SAV2001')");
        System.out.println("  - TTL: key expires after 30 seconds automatically");
        System.out.println("  - Kafka event: other instances consume and invalidate");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Semantic cache invalidation:");
        System.out.println("  - TTL: cached answers expire after 24 hours");
        System.out.println("  - Version tag: when FD rates change, clear rate-related entries");
        System.out.println("  - Never cache volatile or personalized answers");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
CDN invalidation:
  - TTL-based: cache headers (max-age=3600)
  - Purge API: cdn.purge('/static/rates.js')
  - Versioned URLs: /static/rates.v42.js (cache-bust)

Redis invalidation:
  - Explicit delete on write: redis.delete('balance:SAV2001')
  - TTL: key expires after 30 seconds automatically
  - Kafka event: other instances consume and invalidate

Semantic cache invalidation:
  - TTL: cached answers expire after 24 hours
  - Version tag: when FD rates change, clear rate-related entries
  - Never cache volatile or personalized answers
```
